---
/**
 * foodcraft / AreaBM — "Tahanan Cafe" DELIVERY COVERAGE
 * (design element data-component="SERVICE-AREA").
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one coverage panel. A neighbourhood café that runs coffee
 * and pastry out to the streets around it sells that on a plain list of places
 * and — where the owner gave one — how long each run takes. In THIS design the
 * panel is not a tint and not a band: it is the white ground itself, boxed by a
 * single hairline at the design's 2px corner. The grid inside it is drawn with
 * BORDERS, not gaps — the rule under each row IS the separation, which is why
 * the row gap is zero.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html". Not one
 * hex is written in this file (R1); each is named by the token PageBM declares
 * at the same value.
 *   section  padding 62px 30px 0 — the 30px is the design's page gutter, which
 *            is .wrap here, and the 0 bottom is because every sibling section in
 *            this design is padding-top-only. PageBM's shared section rhythm
 *            supplies the trailing gap, so only the 62px ceiling is taken.
 *   panel    NO fill (the white ground shows through) · 1px #EDE6DF all round
 *            · radius 2px · padding 40px 38px      → --line / --r
 *   head     flex · align-items flex-end · space-between · gap 26px ·
 *            flex-wrap wrap · margin-bottom 24px
 *   eyebrow  10px / .34em caps / #7A4E2D / 700 ON a 1px #E0D3C6 underline with
 *            9px of padding — i.e. character-for-character the shared .kick
 *            primitive, so it is NOT restated here. Restating it is how a
 *            section loses this design's most recognisable mark.
 *   h2       Libre Caslon Text 400 · clamp(23px,2.9vw,36px) · 1.18 ·
 *            margin .26em 0 0 · #241B14            → .h2 + .dz-h2 / --ink
 *   note     14.5px / 1.75 / #5C5147 / max-width 34ch / margin 0 → --ink-soft
 *   grid     repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 0 34px (ZERO row gap on purpose)
 *   row      flex · baseline · gap 12px · padding 12px 0 · border-bottom 1px
 *            #F2ECE5                               → .rowrule + --tint-2
 *   place    14.5px / #241B14                      → --ink
 *   time     margin-left auto · 13px / 600 / #7A4E2D / nowrap → --accent
 *
 * THE ROW HAIRLINE IS --tint-2, AND THAT IS AN EXACT MATCH, not a judgement.
 * The design draws the panel's outer box in #EDE6DF (--line) and the rules
 * INSIDE it one step lighter at #F2ECE5, so the frame reads as structure and
 * the row rules read as ledger ruling. PageBM declares #F2ECE5 as --tint-2, so
 * the row overrides exactly one property of the shared .rowrule — its
 * border-bottom-colour — and inherits every metric (flex, baseline, gap 12px,
 * padding 12px 0, min-width 0), which is character-for-character this design's
 * row.
 *
 * WHAT THAT PAIR DOES UNDER AN ADMIN COLOUR PICK — stated correctly, because an
 * earlier draft of this comment had it backwards. The two weights do NOT move
 * together. buildOverrideCss rewrites --line to palette.line with !important and
 * does not touch --tint-2 at all, so an EXPLICIT scheme repaints the frame and
 * leaves the row rules at the design's #F2ECE5; on a dark scheme the inner
 * ruling can end up the BRIGHTER of the two. That is accepted rather than
 * worked around: R1 says map each hex to the token declared at the same value,
 * and re-picking the row colour for whichever token happens to survive the
 * engine is the palette re-derivation that shipped an earlier template in this
 * catalogue looking like a different design. The design's own scheme is the
 * shipped case, and on it the relationship is exact.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "area" — a data-field on any other path is a dead hook no sidebar input
 * owns)
 *   area.tag                — the eyebrow
 *   area.headline           — fed with set:html so an owner can place this
 *                             design's signature italic-walnut emphasis
 *                             anywhere inside their OWN sentence
 *   area.body               — the note beside the headline
 *   area.rows.${i}.place    — the primary list: place + delivery time
 *   area.rows.${i}.time
 *   area.places.${i}        — the FALLBACK list, a list of plain STRINGS.
 *                             Read only when area.rows has no usable row;
 *                             rendered place-only, with no time element emitted
 *                             at all rather than an empty walnut slot.
 *
 * DERIVED / READ BUT NOT HOOKED: area.sub. area.body is the canonical path the
 * schema owns and the only one hooked. area.sub is read as a fallback so a site
 * that stored a paragraph there before the schema settled still shows it; the
 * first edit writes area.body, which then wins, so the two can never disagree
 * on screen. Hooking area.sub would emit a dead hook — v3 gates inline editing
 * on isSchemaEditablePath and the schema does not declare it.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The
 * design's "Delivery" / "Where we deliver", its "Same-day coffee and pastry
 * runs nearby. Office catering needs a day's notice." and its nine Metro Manila
 * districts are the DESIGNER filling in their own template. A same-day promise,
 * a notice period and a 25-minute run to Quezon Ave are operational commitments
 * for a business nobody has spoken to, so none of it ships. A row carrying a
 * time but NO place is dropped whole: a lone walnut "20 min" asserts a delivery
 * to nowhere. A row with a place and no time renders the place alone and the
 * time element is not emitted at all. Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an
 * empty value removes the ELEMENT — never a bare hairline under a row that is
 * not there, never a heading over nothing. The head is guarded at BOTH levels:
 * the whole head only exists when there is a tag, a headline or a note (else
 * its 24px bottom margin opens a hole above the list), and its left half only
 * exists when there is a tag or a headline (an empty flex item under
 * space-between shoves the note to the far edge of the panel).
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes area.tag to
 * 'Service area' and, for any site with a city, area.headline to `Serving
 * ${city}` AND area.places to `[city]` — that is essentially every site we
 * ship. So the gate counts BODY content only, and discounts that one derived
 * entry:
 *   · any usable area.rows row (the schema ships area.rows EMPTY, so a filled
 *     one can only have come from a person), OR
 *   · TWO OR MORE area.places entries (the owner named a second place), OR
 *   · an owner-written area.body.
 * Counting the tag or the headline would print this panel on nearly every build
 * with a single row echoing the café's own city — the address again, dressed up
 * as a delivery zone.
 *
 * KNOWN EDGE, flagged rather than hidden: when the gate opens on area.body
 * alone and area.places is still the derived `[city]`, that one city row is
 * printed. It is true (it is where the café is) and it matches the shipped
 * behaviour of the rest of the catalogue, so consistency wins — but it is the
 * one place a derived value reaches this page, and requiring two places before
 * the list draws at all is a one-line change.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field always carries the ORIGINAL array index, so
 * dropping a blank row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2. This
 * section has no decorative numeral, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * SHAPE TOLERANCE. area.rows is a list of objects; a row that arrived as a
 * plain string is read as its place and still hooked at area.rows.${i}.place,
 * so the first edit heals the row onto the schema key instead of writing a
 * second shape.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately — read these before filing an oversight:
 *   - NO ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER AND NO PAGE_ANCHORS SET (R7). The design gives
 *     SERVICE-AREA no id and no href of any kind, and nothing in this file
 *     emits a link, so an onPage() helper here would be dead code with no call
 *     site — the same omission the BL sibling makes for the same reason. The
 *     page's ids (menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main) are owned by the
 *     sections that actually link to them.
 *   - No button. The design puts no action in this panel, and inventing a
 *     "Order delivery" would be both fabricated copy (R2) and a label this
 *     business never wrote.
 *   - No star row. There is no rating anywhere in this section's data, and R2's
 *     rule stands: stars are drawn only from a numeric rating field.
 *   - No row COUNT asserted in markup or copy. Three tracks because the design
 *     is three tracks; four or eleven owner-typed places lay out on the same
 *     tracks without leaving a hole.
 *   - No map, no radius, no distance unit, no delivery fee, no minimum order.
 *     We have no such data and every one of them is a commercial claim.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY (R9). No image and no .plate here, so no role="img" anywhere.
 * Nothing in this section is decorative — the place and the time are both
 * content and both announced — so nothing is aria-hidden, and no element
 * carries a literal attribute beside a conditional one. The list is a real
 * <ul> AND carries role="list": `list-style: none` is exactly what makes
 * Safari/VoiceOver drop list semantics, so without the role the count never
 * reaches assistive tech.
 *
 * CONTRAST (WCAG 2.x sRGB, the design's palette, measured on --paper — the
 * white ground IS this panel's ground, there is no fill):
 *   --ink on --paper 16.91 (the place) · --ink-soft on --paper 7.71 (the note)
 *   --accent on --paper 7.11 (the eyebrow and the delivery time)
 * Nothing in this section is below AA, at any size, without an override.
 * These three are RE-MEASURED from the hexes, not copied: PageBM's "VERIFIED
 * CONTRAST" table understates the same three pairs (14.9 / 7.29 / 7.06), as it
 * does most of its rows. Every discrepancy there is in the SAFE direction bar
 * band-kick/band (claimed 7.62, actually 7.37 — still far above AA), so nothing
 * has been mis-shipped, but the table is not reproducible from the tokens it
 * names and should not be copied forward into another section.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped style block, no is:global. Scoped rules cannot reach
 * elements that set:html creates, which is why there is no italic-walnut rule
 * for the headline's <em> here: PageBM styles :where(.h2 em) from its global
 * block, so an owner's emphasis is already walnut italic and a scoped duplicate
 * would be dead CSS.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX. `dz-` (delivery zone), distinct from every other BM section and
 * deliberately NOT the `sa-` set the previous template in this family uses —
 * copying a class set verbatim between templates is what an earlier audit had
 * to unpick.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const area = content.area || {};
const tag = area.tag || '';
const headline = area.headline || '';
const body = area.body || area.sub || '';

type Row = { place: string; time: string; placeField: string; timeField: string };

// PRIMARY: area.rows — place + delivery time. An object row is canonical; a
// string row is tolerated and still hooked at .place so the first edit heals it.
const rawRows: any[] = Array.isArray(area.rows) ? area.rows : [];
const timedRows: Row[] = rawRows
  .map((r: any, i: number): Row => {
    const o = r && typeof r === 'object' ? r : { place: r };
    return {
      place: String(o.place ?? '').trim(),
      time: String(o.time ?? '').trim(),
      placeField: `area.rows.${i}.place`,
      timeField: `area.rows.${i}.time`,
    };
  })
  // A time with no place is a delivery to nowhere — drop the whole row (R2).
  .filter((r) => r.place.length > 0);

// FALLBACK: area.places — plain STRINGS, which is how the schema ships them and
// how every other template reads them. Place-only rows; no time element.
const rawPlaces: any[] = Array.isArray(area.places) ? area.places : [];
const placeRows: Row[] = rawPlaces
  .map((p: any, i: number): Row => ({
    place: String(p ?? '').trim(),
    time: '',
    placeField: `area.places.${i}`,
    timeField: '',
  }))
  .filter((r) => r.place.length > 0);

const rows: Row[] = timedRows.length > 0 ? timedRows : placeRows;
// See SECTION GATE in the block above. Never `rows.length > 0`.
const show = timedRows.length > 0 || placeRows.length >= 2 || Boolean(body);
---
{show && (
  <section class="dz">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="dz-frame">
        {(tag || headline || body) && (
          <div class="dz-head reveal">
            {(tag || headline) && (
              <div class="dz-title">
                {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="area.tag">{tag}</span>}
                {headline && <h2 class="h2 dz-h2" data-field="area.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
              </div>
            )}
            {body && <p class="dz-note" data-field="area.body">{body}</p>}
          </div>
        )}

        {rows.length > 0 && (
          <ul class="dz-list reveal" role="list" data-delay="1">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <li class="rowrule dz-row">
                <span class="dz-place" data-field={r.placeField}>{r.place}</span>
                {r.time && <span class="dz-time" data-field={r.timeField}>{r.time}</span>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Design: padding 62px 30px 0 — the 30px gutter is .wrap and the 0 bottom is
     because every sibling in this design is padding-top-only, so PageBM's
     symmetric section rhythm already supplies the trailing gap. Only the 62px
     ceiling is taken, and it is taken on BOTH edges like every other BM section
     — the wrapper made that call for the whole family, and breaking rhythm in
     one section would leave a seam. The 6.2vw slope is not the wrapper's 7vw:
     it is the slope that reaches this section's 46px floor at ~742px, the same
     width at which the wrapper's clamp(52px,7vw,84px) reaches its own, so the
     two shrink in step and this panel never crosses over a neighbour on the way
     down. Beats `html[data-page="BM"] section` (0,1,2) at (0,2,0) via Astro's
     scope attribute (scopedStyleStrategy defaults to 'attribute' in Astro 5, so
     the scope really does add a class-weight unit — it would NOT under the
     'where' strategy, and every specificity note in this file assumes the
     default) — no !important needed. */
  .dz { padding: clamp(46px, 6.2vw, 62px) 0; }

  /* Design: NO fill · 1px #EDE6DF all round · 2px corner · padding 40px 38px.
     The absence of a background is the design — this panel is the white ground
     boxed by a hairline, which is why the row rules inside it have to be a
     lighter weight than the frame to read as ruling rather than as more frame.
     The clamps resolve to exactly 40px / 38px at every width down to ~705px,
     below which 38px of padding inside a 92vw wrap starts eating the rows. */
  .dz-frame {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    padding: clamp(22px, 5.7vw, 40px) clamp(18px, 5.4vw, 38px);
  }

  /* Design: flex · flex-end · space-between · gap 26px · wrap · mb 24px. */
  .dz-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 26px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 on both halves so a long unbroken owner string shrinks its half
     instead of widening the panel and pushing the page sideways (R8). */
  .dz-title { min-width: 0; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .34em tracking, so one
     unbroken word is unusually wide for its character count (R8). ONLY these
     two properties are stated: the underline, the colour, the size and the
     tracking all stay on the shared .kick primitive, because restating the
     eyebrow is how a section in this template loses the design's signature
     rule. .kick is an inline-block, so max-width:100% is what bounds it. */
  .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: Libre Caslon Text 400 · clamp(23px,2.9vw,36px) · 1.18 ·
     margin .26em 0 0. DELIBERATELY smaller than the wrapper's .h2
     (clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px)) and with the margin on the TOP rather than the
     bottom — the design sizes this panel head below a full section head and
     hangs it off the eyebrow above it. The wrapper's rule is a :where(), so
     this class wins at (0,2,0) with no !important.
     max-width:none — the design's SERVICE-AREA h2 carries NO measure cap and
     sits on one line beside the 34ch note; the wrapper's `.h2 { max-width:
     24ch }` would break it. Releasing it cannot widen the panel, because
     .dz-title is a flex item at min-width:0 and this rule carries
     overflow-wrap, so a long owner headline shrinks and breaks instead (R8).
     CLAMP CHECK: this is the section's ONLY font-size for the h2 — no
     breakpoint below restates it, so it falls monotonically from 36px to its
     23px floor at ~793px and stays there. It cannot grow as the viewport
     narrows. (The wrapper's 760px `:where(.h2)` bump to clamp(26px,7vw,38px)
     is (0,1,1) and loses to this rule, which is the intent: this is a panel
     head, not a section head, and it must not overtake the sections around
     it on a phone.) */
  .dz-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(23px, 2.9vw, 36px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    margin: 0.26em 0 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: 14.5px / 1.75 / #5C5147 / 34ch / margin 0. Same COLOUR as the
     wrapper's .body primitive but not the same scale — .body clamps to
     14.5–15px/1.8 and sets no measure — so the metrics are stated here and the
     colour still comes from the token. (0,2,0) beats the wrapper's (0,1,1)
     bare-paragraph margin reset, so `margin: 0` actually holds. */
  .dz-note {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 0 34px. The ZERO row gap is the
     design: the hairline under each row is the separation, and a row gap would
     open a space between a rule and the row beneath it. minmax(0,1fr) rather
     than 1fr so a long place name cannot widen its own track. */
  .dz-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 34px;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* Flex, baseline, 12px gap, 12px 0 padding and min-width:0 all come from the
     wrapper's .rowrule, which is character-for-character this design's row.
     Only the list-item chrome and the ONE property the design states
     differently — the lighter #F2ECE5 rule, which is --tint-2 exactly — are set
     here. See THE ROW HAIRLINE in the frontmatter. */
  .dz-row {
    list-style: none;
    border-bottom-color: var(--tint-2);
  }

  .dz-place {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Design: margin-left auto · 13px · 600 · #7A4E2D · nowrap. No font-family:
     the design leaves the time on Karla, so this is NOT .fig — .fig is the
     serif figure this template reserves for prices and proof numerals. */
  .dz-time {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design and they
     must not survive past 900px, where a 34px gutter leaves a place name and
     its time fighting over the same line. Nothing in these queries restates a
     font-size that a wider query also sets, so no clamp can invert. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .dz-list { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0 26px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .dz-list { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .dz-head { gap: 18px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
    .dz-note { max-width: none; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* Release the design's nowrap at the one width where a long owner-typed
       time ("about 25 minutes on a good day") would push the viewport sideways.
       Above 520px the tracks are wide enough for it to hold on one line. */
    .dz-time { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; text-align: right; }
  }
</style>
